Magnesium is a mineral that helps turn the food we eat into energy (NHS). It also helps the parathyroid glands work normally, which is important for bone health (NHS). It is needed for healthy bones, muscle development and nerve function (Leeds NHS). Good food sources include spinach, nuts, wholemeal bread, wholegrain breakfast cereals, sardines, cheese, white bread and yeast extract (NHS, Leeds NHS). The recommended daily intake for adult men is 300 mg and for adult women 270 mg (NHS). Taking high doses of magnesium (more than 400 mg) for a short time can cause diarrhoea (NHS). Intravenous magnesium concentrations are limited to 20 % (0.2 g/mL or 0.8 mmol/mL) for central lines, 5 % (0.05 g/mL or 0.2 mmol/mL) for peripheral lines, and the infusion rate should not exceed 9 g per hour (NHS SPS). Magnesium glycerophosphate tablets and liquids are unlicensed preparations that may be prescribed on a special‑order basis (NICE/BNF).

