Magnesium is a mineral that helps turn the food we eat into energy and helps ensure the parathyroid glands work normally, according to NHS [https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/vitamins-and-minerals/others](https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/vitamins-and-minerals/others).  
Food sources include spinach, nuts, wholemeal bread, and whole‑grain breakfast cereals such as Cheerios, Shreddies, Shredded Wheat and Weetabix, as listed in NHS patient leaflets [https://www.hey.nhs.uk/patient-leaflet/increasing-magnesium-in-your-diet](https://www.hey.nhs.uk/patient-leaflet/increasing-magnesium-in-your-diet).  
The NHS recommends an intake of about 300 mg per day for men and 270 mg per day for women aged 19 to 64 [https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/vitamins-and-minerals/others](https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/vitamins-and-minerals/others).  
NHS notes that taking more than 400 mg may cause diarrhoea [https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/vitamins-and-minerals/others](https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/vitamins-and-minerals/others).  
