According to the HRA, iron is essential for the synthesis of proteins that regulate cell growth and division and for DNA formation (https://www.hra.nhs.uk/planning-and-improving-research/application-summaries/research-summaries/is-iron-in-the-driving-seat-for-sarcoma). Kingston and Richmond NHS states that iron is needed to make haemoglobin, which carries oxygen in the blood, and that it occurs in animal foods such as liver, kidney, red meat and sardines, and in plant foods including wholegrain cereals, fortified breakfast cereals, pulse vegetables, dark green vegetables, nuts and dried fruit (https://www.kingstonandrichmond.nhs.uk/patients-and-families/patient-leaflets/iron-diet-sheet). The NHS indicates that the recommended daily intake is 8.7 mg for men and for women over 50, and 14.8 mg for women aged 19 to 49 (https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/vitamins-and-minerals/iron).

